{"id":"15428796-8e9f-4685-b9d8-a3093c6687e2","object":"chat.completion","created":1785033361,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    nodes_by_id = {}\\n    children_by_id = {}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        nodes_by_id[nid] = n\\n        children_by_id[nid] = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children_by_id[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    # postorder traversal to compute subtree_nodes and subtree_symbol_counts\\n    subtree_nodes = {}\\n    subtree_symbol_counts = {}\\n    stack = [(root_id, 0)]\\n    while stack:\\n        nid, state = stack.pop()\\n        if state == 0:\\n            stack.append((nid, 1))\\n            for child in reversed(children_by_id[nid]):\\n                stack.append((child, 0))\\n        else:\\n            nodes_set = {nid}\\n            sym_counts = {nodes_by_id[nid]['symbol']: 1}\\n            for child in children_by_id[nid]:\\n                child_set = subtree_nodes[child]\\n                child_counts = subtree_symbol_counts[child]\\n                nodes_set.update(child_set)\\n                for sym, cnt in child_counts.items():\\n                    sym_counts[sym] = sym_counts.get(sym, 0) + cnt\\n            subtree_nodes[nid] = nodes_set\\n            subtree_symbol_counts[nid] = sym_counts\\n    total_counts = subtree_symbol_counts[root_id].copy()\\n    retained_set = set(nodes_by_id.keys())\\n    removable_candidates = []\\n    for nid, n in nodes_by_id.items():\\n        if n['removable'] and nid != root_id:\\n            removable_candidates.append(nid)\\n    removable_candidates.sort(key=lambda nid: (-nodes_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for nid in removable_candidates:\\n        if nid not in retained_set:\\n            continue\\n        # check if any descendant retained\\n        if any(desc in retained_set for desc in subtree_nodes[nid] if desc != nid):\\n            continue\\n        # check required symbols after removal\\n        sub_counts = subtree_symbol_counts[nid]\\n        can_remove = True\\n        for sym, cnt in sub_counts.items():\\n            if sym in required_symbols:\\n                if total_counts.get(sym, 0) - cnt == 0:\\n                    can_remove = False\\n                    break\\n        if not can_remove:\\n            continue\\n        # remove subtree\\n        for snid in subtree_nodes[nid]:\\n            retained_set.discard(snid)\\n            total_counts[nodes_by_id[snid]['symbol']] -= 1\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained_set)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_ids}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The problem description mentions \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" and \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\". The input payload contains a tree of nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. The task is to remove removable nodes while preserving required symbols. The output should have removed_node_ids (list of IDs removed in removal order) and retained_node_ids (list of IDs that remain, sorted). \n\nKey points from instructions:\n- Must not remove root, required symbols, or nodes with retained children. (A node with retained children means if a node's descendant is retained, then the node must be retained? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Means if a node has children that are retained, you cannot remove the node because its children depend on it. But if all children are removed, then the node might be removable if it's removable itself and not required, etc.)\n- \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So after removal, the set of symbols in the retained subtree must still cover all required_symbols.\n- Use depth: deeper nodes before shallower nodes.\n- Use node ID as tie break at equal depth.\n- Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nWe need to parse the tree, identify removable nodes (removable=true), and try to remove them in order (deeper first, then by ID). For each removal candidate, we need to check if removing it would cause loss of a required symbol. Also need to ensure that we do not remove a node if it has retained children, i.e., if any descendant is retained, then the node must remain because it's an ancestor. But that's automatically enforced if we consider that removing a node also removes its subtree. So if we remove a node, we remove all its descendants. So we must ensure that before removing a node, none of its descendants are kept. That is, we can only remove a node if its entire subtree is removable (or will be removed). So a depth-first removal order from leaves upward works.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Build a mapping from id to node dictionary.\n2. Build children list for each node (parent).\n3. Identify root id (parent null).\n4. Initialize a set of retained node IDs to all nodes (or we can start with all, then remove).\n5. Determine the order of removable nodes to consider: filter nodes where removable=true. Sort by depth descending (deeper first), then by id ascending.\n6. For each candidate node in that order:\n   - Check if it's still in retained set (might have been removed already if parent removed).\n   - Check if any of its descendants are still retained (i.e., if any node in its subtree is retained). Since we process deeper first, we should have already considered all descendants. But if a descendant is not removable (or we decided to keep it), then we cannot remove this ancestor. So condition: after removal of this node, the entire subtree goes away. So we need to ensure that all nodes in the subtree are already removed or will be removed (i.e., they are not retained). So we can check: for each node in subtree, if it's in retained set, then we cannot remove ancestor. But since we process from leaves, if a descendant is removable and we already removed it, it's fine. If a descendant is not removable (removable=false), then we cannot remove the ancestor because that descendant would be lost. Also if a descendant is removable but we haven't processed it yet (because it's at same depth? But we process deeper first, so all deeper are processed before. However, if two nodes at same depth? They are processed in ID order, but if one is a descendant deeper, it's processed before. So no issue. For same depth nodes that are siblings, removal of one does not affect the other. So condition: node's subtree must contain only nodes that are either not retained (already removed) or are candidates that we will also remove? But we can only allow removal if after removal, required symbols still exist elsewhere. So we need to check condition per node.\n\nBetter approach: start with all nodes retained. Then for each removable node in the specified order (depth desc, id asc), simulate removing it: if removal would cause the retained subtree (excluding this node and its descendants) to still cover all required symbols, and also it's valid (node is removable and not required and not root), then remove it (i.e., remove it and all its descendants from retained set). But careful: if a descendant is also removable and we haven't considered it yet (since we process deeper first, all descendants are deeper, so they are considered before ancestors). So by the time we consider an ancestor, all its descendants have already been either removed or kept. So if a descendant was kept (maybe because required symbol or not removable), then the ancestor cannot be removed. So we can check: if any descendant is still retained, do not remove ancestor. That is correct.\n\nAlso we must ensure that removal does not cause required symbol loss. So check that after removing the subtree, the set of symbols in retained nodes (excluding the subtree) still includes all required_symbols. This can be done by precomputing symbol coverage.\n\nWe can maintain a set of retained IDs, and a dictionary mapping symbol to count of nodes with that symbol in retained set. When we consider removing a node, we need to know if its subtree contains any required symbol that would be unique (i.e., that symbol's count becomes zero after removal). Count contributions of symbols in subtree. For each symbol in subtree, if its count in retained minus count in subtree becomes zero, and that symbol is required, then cannot remove.\n\nAlso need to consider that removing a node removes its descendants, so we must subtract all symbols in subtree.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Parse case: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n- Build dict nodes_by_id, children_by_id.\n- Find root id.\n- Precompute for each node the set of symbols in its subtree? Or we can compute symbol counts on the fly. Since number of nodes is likely small, we can do BFS/DFS from each candidate to compute subtree symbols. But we can also precompute subtree symbol counts per node to avoid repeated traversals. We'll compute subtree symbol count dictionary for each node (mapping symbol to count in that subtree). Also total symbol counts for the whole tree.\n\n- Initialize retained_set = set of all node IDs.\n- Initialize total_symbol_counts = counts across all nodes.\n- Order removable nodes: sort by depth descending, then id ascending.\n- For each candidate node in order:\n   - If node id not in retained_set: continue (already removed).\n   - If node is root (parent null): skip (root not removable anyway).\n   - If any descendant of node is in retained_set? Actually we check retained_set for descendants: we can compute subtree nodes (we can have a precomputed list of descendants per node). If any descendant in retained_set, skip.\n   - Compute subtree_symbol_counts for node (precomputed). For each symbol s, if total_symbol_counts[s] - subtree_symbol_counts[s] == 0 and s in required_symbols, then removal would lose required symbol -> skip.\n   - Else, remove node and all its descendants from retained_set: remove each id in the subtree (including node). Update total_symbol_counts by subtracting subtree_symbol_counts.\n   - Record node id as removed (in order).\n- After processing all, remaining retained_set are retained nodes. Sort them alphabetically (string) for output (since IDs are strings like \"root\", \"n1\", etc.). Also removed_node_ids should be in the order we removed them.\n\n- Output: {\"removed_node_ids\": list, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted list}\n\nEdge cases: root is not removable and required (since required_symbols includes \"root\" in examples). So it will never be removed. Also required \"s1\" appears multiple times, so removal of some s1 nodes may be allowed if another s1 remains.\n\nWe must also respect \"Never remove a node with retained children\". This is covered by checking if any descendant is retained.\n\nOne nuance: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we check that after removal, all required symbols still exist.\n\nNow implement solve(case) function. Use Python standard library.\n\nWe need to generate a JSON response with \"implementation\" key containing the source code as a JSON string. We'll indent the code nicely but must escape properly.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nI'll define a class Node or just use dictionaries. I'll precompute ancestors? Not needed.\n\nWe'll compute for each node its descendants (set of IDs) recursively. Since the tree is small, we can use recursion.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    nodes_by_id = {}\n    children_by_id = {}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        nodes_by_id[nid] = n\n        children_by_id[nid] = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children_by_id[pid].append(n['id'])\n    # Precompute subtree nodes and symbol counts\n    def dfs(nid):\n        subtree_nodes = {nid}\n        symbol_counts = {}\n        n = nodes_by_id[nid]\n        symbol_counts[n['symbol']] = 1\n        for child_id in children_by_id[nid]:\n            child_subtree, child_counts = dfs(child_id)\n            subtree_nodes.update(child_subtree)\n            for sym, cnt in child_counts.items():\n                symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + cnt\n        return subtree_nodes, symbol_counts\n    # Compute for all nodes\n    subtree_nodes = {}\n    subtree_symbol_counts = {}\n    # Use iterative stack to avoid recursion depth issues? But small.\n    # We'll do DFS from root.\n    stack = [(root_id, 0)]  # state 0: entering, 1: exiting\n    order = []\n    while stack:\n        nid, state = stack.pop()\n        if state == 0:\n            stack.append((nid, 1))\n            for child in reversed(children_by_id[nid]):\n                stack.append((child, 0))\n        else:\n            # post-order\n            nodes_set = {nid}\n            sym_counts = {nodes_by_id[nid]['symbol']: 1}\n            for child in children_by_id[nid]:\n                child_set = subtree_nodes[child]\n                child_counts = subtree_symbol_counts[child]\n                nodes_set.update(child_set)\n                for sym, cnt in child_counts.items():\n                    sym_counts[sym] = sym_counts.get(sym, 0) + cnt\n            subtree_nodes[nid] = nodes_set\n            subtree_symbol_counts[nid] = sym_counts\n    # Total counts\n    total_counts = subtree_symbol_counts[root_id].copy()\n    retained_set = set(nodes_by_id.keys())\n    # Sort removable nodes\n    removable_candidates = []\n    for nid, n in nodes_by_id.items():\n        if n['removable'] and nid != root_id:\n            removable_candidates.append(nid)\n    removable_candidates.sort(key=lambda nid: (-nodes_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\n    removed_order = []\n    for nid in removable_candidates:\n        if nid not in retained_set:\n            continue\n        # Check if any descendant retained\n        if any(desc in retained_set for desc in subtree_nodes[nid] if desc != nid):\n            continue  # has retained children\n        # Check required symbols\n        sub_counts = subtree_symbol_counts[nid]\n        # Simulate removal: check if any required symbol becomes zero\n        can_remove = True\n        for sym, cnt in sub_counts.items():\n            if sym in required_symbols:\n                if total_counts.get(sym, 0) - cnt == 0:\n                    can_remove = False\n                    break\n        if not can_remove:\n            continue\n        # Remove subtree\n        sub_nodes = subtree_nodes[nid]\n        for snid in sub_nodes:\n            retained_set.discard(snid)\n            # Update total_counts\n            sn = nodes_by_id[snid]\n            total_counts[sn['symbol']] -= 1\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained_set)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nWe need to ensure we don't mutate case. We don't.\n\nNow output the entire response as JSON with \"implementation\" key.\n\nLet's write the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2142,"completion_tokens":3404,"total_tokens":5546,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2698},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":94},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}